        content: `<p>Commercial property values are cyclical. UK commercial property fell approximately 40% from peak to trough during the 2007–09 financial crisis, and experienced a further 20% correction in 2022–23 as interest rates rose sharply. A SIPP holding commercial property during such a period will see its headline value fall — but the impact on pension functionality depends heavily on whether the property is let, whether it is mortgaged, and what other assets the SIPP holds.</p>
<p>A fully let property on a long lease continues generating rental income regardless of what its capital value is doing. The SIPP's income — and therefore its ability to service mortgage debt and fund drawdown — is unaffected by a capital value decline, provided the tenant remains in occupation. The practical problem arises when capital value falls intersect with other pressures: lease expiry, mortgage maturity, or a pension holder who needs to sell at precisely the wrong time.</p>`,
